WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.535
Here is a cart called c1.

2
00:00:04.535 --> 00:00:08.132
Its skus field holds a list with one item, sku-1.

3
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:10.735
Copy it, and change only the id.

4
00:00:10.735 --> 00:00:13.061
Now there is a second cart, c2.

5
00:00:13.267 --> 00:00:16.225
The copy takes each field's value as it is.

6
00:00:16.225 --> 00:00:18.840
But the value of skus is not the list.

7
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.698
It is an arrow to the list.

8
00:00:20.698 --> 00:00:23.725
So c2 gets a second arrow, to the same list.

9
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:26.199
Now add sku-2 to c2's list.

10
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.284
What does c1's list hold now?

11
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:30.877
Both items.

12
00:00:30.877 --> 00:00:32.202
Nobody touched c1.

13
00:00:32.202 --> 00:00:36.252
But there is only one list, and both carts point at it.

14
00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:37.984
This is a shallow copy.

15
00:00:37.984 --> 00:00:41.941
It copies the fields, and shares every object they point to.

16
00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:45.644
Kotlin's copy() works this way, and its docs say so.

17
00:00:45.644 --> 00:00:48.952
So does a Java record wither, and Object.clone().

18
00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:54.752
So a copy is shallow until you make it deep, one nested object at a time.

19
00:00:54.752 --> 00:00:57.791
The simple way: store a list that can't change.

20
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.460
Now c2 can't add to the shared list.

21
00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:05.829
To change c2's items, you build a new list: the old items plus sku-2.

22
00:01:05.829 --> 00:01:07.400
Only c2 points to it.

23
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.953
And c1 still holds one item.

24
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:15.621
In Kotlin, make the field a read-only List and change it with plus.

25
00:01:15.621 --> 00:01:21.984
In Java, wrap it with List.copyOf in the compact constructor, and let withers build a new list.

26
00:01:22.933 --> 00:01:26.240
Building an object from scratch has a different trap.

27
00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:27.363
Look at this call.

28
00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:29.344
Which value is the timeout?

29
00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:31.795
You have to count.

30
00:01:31.795 --> 00:01:36.563
The Java fix is a builder: a second class with one method per field.

31
00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:38.827
Kotlin fixes it in the language.

32
00:01:38.827 --> 00:01:42.689
Arguments can carry names, and parameters can have defaults.

33
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:48.313
So the call sets the four values it needs, in any order, and the defaults fill the rest.

34
00:01:48.533 --> 00:01:50.653
copy() gives you a changed copy.

35
00:01:50.653 --> 00:01:54.627
And checks in init run on every construction, copy included.

36
00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:56.774
Java has no named arguments.

37
00:01:56.774 --> 00:02:00.863
If Java code calls the class a lot, give it a real builder.

38
00:02:01.933 --> 00:02:02.521
The rule.

39
00:02:02.521 --> 00:02:05.589
A copy shares every object its fields point to.

40
00:02:05.589 --> 00:02:10.551
Keep those objects unchangeable, and build a new one when you need a change.

41
00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:11.738
Quick check.

42
00:02:11.738 --> 00:02:15.172
A Payment holds a MutableMap of metadata.

43
00:02:15.172 --> 00:02:20.448
You make p2 with p1.copy(), then put a retry key into p2's map.

44
00:02:20.448 --> 00:02:21.704
Does p1 see it?

45
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:24.396
Yes.

46
00:02:24.396 --> 00:02:27.136
copy() copied the arrow, not the map.

47
00:02:27.136 --> 00:02:29.432
Both payments point at one map.
